Backport: close the two sandbox escapes (str.format and the attr filter)
========================================================================

Target:   jinja2 2.11.3 (tag 2.11.3, pallets/jinja)
Fixes:    CVE-2024-56326  (indirect reference to str.format;  upstream fix in 3.1.5)
          CVE-2025-27516  (attr filter selects format;        upstream fix in 3.1.6)

Upstream commits this is derived from:
  48b0687e  sandbox indirect calls to str.format
  90457bbf  attr filter uses env.getattr

Apply AFTER 0001. Both patches touch filters.py; in the other order the
do_attr hunk does not apply.

What the two fixes actually do
------------------------------
2.11.3 sandboxes str.format at CALL time: SandboxedEnvironment.call() sniffs
the callable with inspect_format_method() and reroutes it through
format_string(). Anything that obtains the bound format method and calls it
WITHOUT going through call() therefore escapes the sandbox - which is
CVE-2024-56326. Upstream's answer is to sandbox at ACCESS time instead: getattr
and getitem now wrap a str.format they are about to hand out, and the call-time
hook is deleted.

That relocation is what creates the second bug. do_attr used a raw getattr(),
so once the call-time hook is gone, `x|attr("format")` hands back an unwrapped
method - CVE-2025-27516. The fix routes do_attr through environment.getattr(),
using inspect.getattr_static to check existence first so env.getattr's
obj[name] fallback cannot be triggered by accident.

The two must land together. Applying only the sandbox half would INTRODUCE
CVE-2025-27516 into 2.11.3 - verified: the upstream PoC for it is already
blocked on unpatched 2.11.3, precisely because the call-time hook catches it.

Where this deviates from upstream, and why
------------------------------------------
wrap_str_format delegates to 2.11.3's existing format_string() rather than
porting 3.1.x's reimplemented body. The vulnerability is WHEN sandboxing
happens, not how the formatting is done, and 2.11.3's formatter carries
_MagicFormatMapping for auto-numbered fields which 3.1.x no longer needs.
Reusing the tested local path keeps the diff to the security change.

Type annotations are dropped throughout (2.11.3 has none - it still claimed
Python 2.7 support) and string_types is used in place of `str`.

Verification
------------
Upstream PoCs, verbatim from the two fix commits, on ubi9/python-312 with
markupsafe 2.0.1:

  unpatched 2.11.3   CVE-2024-56326 EXPLOITED - renders
                     '<built-in function __import__>'
  patched            all four CVEs blocked (SecurityError / ValueError)

jinja2 2.11.3's own test suite, 747 tests, patched vs pristine: failure sets
IDENTICAL, 14 failures in both, stable across repeated runs. Those 14 are
2.11.3's own Python 3.12 incompatibilities, not this patch.

Two traps if you re-verify this yourself. jinja 2.11.3's setup.cfg sets
`filterwarnings = error`, so a tree WITH setup.cfg reports ~105 more failures
than one without - compare like with like. And under that setting the
markupsafe soft_unicode DeprecationWarning attaches to whichever test first
triggers a deferred import, so the failure set moves by one test between runs
on identical code. Run pytest with -W ignore::DeprecationWarning to compare
reliably.

